"Plots each observation’s Cook’s distance to reveal how strongly removing it could change the fitted regression.\n\nUse when: A fitted regression requires case-influence screening. Readers need to identify specific records for sensitivity analysis.\n\nAvoid when: Influence values are used as automatic deletion rules without domain review.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal position identifies the observation or its acquisition order. Stem height encodes nonnegative Cook’s distance under the fitted model.\n\nBuild: Compute Cook’s distances using the correct fitted model and residual variance. Draw one stem per included observation with enough space for selected identifiers. Add any chosen heuristic reference with its exact formula and interpretation.\n\nCheck: Check identifiers map back to the original records correctly. Verify Cook’s distances are nonnegative and finite where defined. Re-fit selected sensitivity cases rather than assuming a threshold guarantees a material effect.\n\nPitfalls: A large distance does not prove a data error or justify removing an observation. Reference rules such as 4/n are heuristics and should not be presented as universal significance boundaries.\n\nExample: Screen synthetic regression records for influential cases, then compare fitted coefficients with and without the two largest-distance records.\n\nAlternatives: Leverage plot, Residual plot, Dot plot."
